## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a recurrence for a simple constraint (no adjacent picks), then implement both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation. Requires correct base cases for N=0 and N=1 and translating to iterative state transitions. Follow-up prompts space optimization and practical coding habits.

## Key Concepts
- DP for non-adjacent max sum (House Robber)
- Top-down memoization with base cases
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Space optimization to O(1)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxNonAdjacent(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  const memo = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const solve = i => {
    if (i < 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    memo[i] = Math.max(nums[i] + solve(i-2), solve(i-1));
    return memo[i];
  };
  return solve(n-1);
}
function maxNonAdjacentTab(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  let prev2 = 0, prev1 = Math.max(0, nums[0]);
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++){
    const cur = Math.max(prev1, nums[i] + prev2);
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }
  return prev1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative values or all-negative arrays?
- How would you adapt to a similar constraint with k-adjacent bans (no more than two chosen in any window of size k)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with dual objectives: minimize cost and count optimal deletion sets, plus implement in two styles. It also covers edge cases like empty substrings and ties.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings; store (cost, ways).
- Memoization and bottom-up parallels; careful counting on ties.
- Modulo arithmetic for counts.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Memoized DFS
function solveMemo(s, delCost){
  const n = s.length;
  const memoCost = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(-1));
  const memoWays = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  function dfs(l,r){
    if (l>=r) return {cost:0, ways:1};
    if (memoCost[l][r]!==-1) return {cost:memoCost[l][r], ways:memoWays[l][r]};
    if (s[l]===s[r]){
      const res = dfs(l+1,r-1);
      memoCost[l][r]=res.cost;
      memoWays[l][r]=res.ways;
      return res;
    } else {
      const A = dfs(l+1,r);
      const B = dfs(l,r-1);
      let cost;
      let ways = 0;
      if (A.cost + delCost[l] < B.cost + delCost[r]){
        cost = A.cost + delCost[l];
        ways = A.ways;
      } else if (B.cost + delCost[r] < A.cost + delCost[l]){
        cost = B.cost + delCost[r];
        ways = B.ways;
      } else {
        cost = A.cost + Math.min(delCost[l], delCost[r]);
        ways = (A.ways + B.ways) % MOD;
      }
      memoCost[l][r]=cost; memoWays[l][r]=ways;
      return {cost, ways};
    }
  }
  return dfs(0,n-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of graph algorithms, state expansion techniques, and path reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm on expanded state space (node, used)
- Edge relaxation with and without discount application
- Path reconstruction across state transitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestWithDiscounts(n, edges, s, t, K) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>[]);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){ adj[u].push([v,w]); }
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(null));

  const pq = new MinHeap();
  dist[s][0] = 0;
  pq.insert([s,0,0]); // [node, used, distance]

  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [u,used,d] = pq.extractMin();
    if(d > dist[u][used]) continue;
    if(u === t) break;

    for(const [v,w] of adj[u]){
      // Without discount
      if(d + w < dist[v][used]){
        dist[v][used] = d + w;
        prev[v][used] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used,d+w]);
      }
      // With discount if available
      if(used < K && d < dist[v][used+1]){
        dist[v][used+1] = d;
        prev[v][used+1] = [u,used];
        pq.insert([v,used+1,d]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Find optimal state at target
  let minDist = INF, minUsed = -1;
  for(let k=0;k<=K;k++){
    if(dist[t][k] < minDist){
      minDist = dist[t][k];
      minUsed = k;
    }
  }

  // Path reconstruction
  const path = [];
  let curr = [t,minUsed];
  while(curr && prev[curr[0]][curr[1]]){
    path.unshift(curr[0]);
    curr = prev[curr[0]][curr[1]];
  }
  if(curr[0] === s) path.unshift(s);

  return {distance: minDist, path};
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Unreachable target: return INF distance
- K=0: reduces to standard Dijkstra
- K larger than path length: effectively unlimited discounts
- Multiple optimal states: choose minimal used count

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple target nodes efficiently?
- How does the solution change if edges can have zero weight, or if counts exceed 64-bit, and you need a different modulus or exact arithmetic?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you empirically determine the optimal threshold size?
- What techniques would you use to verify the algorithm's stability?
- How does this hybrid approach perform on partially sorted data?
- What modifications would be needed for parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked

This question combines multiple data structure concepts: per-user sliding windows within a global top-K context. It integrates arrays (per-user queues), hash tables for aggregates, and heaps for top-K retrieval with lazy invalidation. The problem tests a candidate's ability to:
- Design composite data structures for streaming workloads
- Handle time-based expirations efficiently
- Reason about algorithmic complexity under constraints
- Manage edge cases in production systems

## Key Concepts

1. **Sliding window per user** — Each user maintains their own event history, allowing O(1) expiration of old events via queue front-draining.
2. **Lazy invalidation** — The heap never explicitly deletes stale entries; instead, we check and skip them during retrieval. This keeps `addEvent` at O(log n) without expensive heap repairs.
3. **Aggregate maps** — Fast O(1) lookup and update of per-user totals, avoiding full recalculation.
4. **Tie-breaking** — Using `(delta, user_id)` as a composite key ensures stable, deterministic ordering.

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingActiveUsers {
  constructor(windowSeconds) {
    this.W = windowSeconds;
    this.userWindows = new Map();
    this.userTotals = new Map();
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap();
  }

  addEvent(ts, userId, delta) {
    if (!this.userWindows.has(userId)) {
      this.userWindows.set(userId, []);
      this.userTotals.set(userId, 0);
    }
    this.userWindows.get(userId).push({ ts, delta });
    const newTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId) + delta;
    this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);
    this.maxHeap.push({ delta: newTotal, userId });
    this.expireIfNeeded(userId, ts);
  }

  expireIfNeeded(userId, now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    const events = this.userWindows.get(userId);
    while (events.length && events[0].ts < cutoff) {
      const expired = events.shift();
      this.userTotals.set(userId, this.userTotals.get(userId) - expired.delta);
    }
  }

  topKUsers(k, now) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      const top = this.maxHeap.pop();
      if (!top || !this.isValid(top, now)) break;
      results.push(top.userId);
      this.maxHeap.push(top);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isValid(entry, now) {
    const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(entry.userId);
    return currentTotal !== undefined && Math.abs(currentTotal - entry.delta) < 0.0001;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis

| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `addEvent` | O(log n) | O(n) |
| `topKUsers` | O(K log n) | O(K) |
| Expiration | O(m) amortized per event, where m = events expired | — |

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to re-push extracted heap entries** — corrupts the heap state
- **Not handling negative totals** — users with negative deltas are valid
- **Skipping expiration check in `topKUsers`** — stale heap entries will produce wrong results
- **Memory leaks** — empty deques should be removed from the map

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design augmented self-balancing BSTs for dynamic range queries—a common requirement in analytics and data processing applications.

## Key Concepts

- **AVL balancing**: Maintaining height balance factor (|left.height - right.height| ≤ 1) through rotations
- **Augmented BST data**: Storing additional aggregate information (cnt, sum) at each node
- **Range query on BST**: Using the BST property to efficiently prune irrelevant subtrees
- **Correctness and complexity analysis**: Ensuring O(log n) operations and verifying invariants

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(key) {
    this.key = key;
    this.cnt = 1;
    this.height = 1;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
    this.sum = key;
  }
}

function getHeight(node) { return node ? node.height : 0; }
function getSum(node) { return node ? node.sum : 0; }
function update(node) {
  node.height = 1 + Math.max(getHeight(node.left), getHeight(node.right));
  node.sum = getSum(node.left) + node.key * node.cnt + getSum(node.right);
}

function rightRotate(y) {
  const x = y.left;
  y.left = x.right;
  x.right = y;
  update(y);
  update(x);
  return x;
}

function rangeSum(node, a, b) {
  if (!node) return 0;
  if (node.key < a) return rangeSum(node.right, a, b);
  if (node.key > b) return rangeSum(node.left, a, b);
  return getSum(node.left) + node.key * node.cnt + rangeSum(node.right, a, b);
}
```

